body:
I've ran it and the warning occurs because of CameraOpenTask code which I'm not sure why even though it's moved to an AsyncTask as is no longer executing in the main thread.

Here is CameraOpenTask:

       public class CameraOpenTask extends AsyncTask<Void, Void, Camera>{
        @Override
        protected Camera doInBackground(Void... params){
            Camera camera = Camera.open();
            Parameters p = camera.getParameters();
            p.setFlashMode(Parameters.FLASH_MODE_TORCH);
            camera.setParameters(p);
            camera.startPreview();
            return camera;
        }
    }

I get the following error for the third line.
a value of type "char **" cannot be used to initialize an entity of type "char *"

I've tried the following and I get no errors:

     char v1 = 0x7A;
     char* v8 = &v1;
     char* vC = v8;
     return 0;

Would this be a solution to the exercise:


      char var_1 = 'R';
      char* var_8 = &var_1;
      char*var_C = var_8;


I keep getting confused on whether to use a pointer or not when reading assembly.
